Command	Description
pwd	Displays the current working directory.
ls	Lists files and directories.
cd	Changes the current directory.
mkdir	Creates a new directory.
rmdir	Removes an empty directory.
touch	Creates a new empty file.
cp	Copies files or directories.
mv	Moves or renames files and directories.
rm	Removes files or directories.
cat	Displays the contents of a file.
less	Views a file one page at a time.
head	Displays the beginning of a file.
tail	Displays the end of a file.
grep	Searches for text patterns in files or command output.
find	Searches for files and directories.
chmod	Changes file or directory permissions.
chown	Changes the owner of a file or directory.
sudo	Runs a command with elevated privileges.
su	Switches to another user account.
ps	Lists currently running processes.
kill	Terminates a running process.
top	Displays running processes and system resource usage.
df	Displays available disk space.
du	Displays disk usage of files and directories.
ip	Displays or configures network information.
ping	Tests network connectivity to a host.
ssh	Connects to a remote computer securely.
wget	Downloads files from the web.
curl	Transfers data to or from a server.
tar	Creates or extracts archive files.
man	Displays the manual/documentation for a command.
clear	Clears the terminal screen.
history	Displays previously executed commands.
echo	Prints text or variable values to the terminal.
apt	Installs, updates, or removes packages on Debian-based systems.
shutdown	Shuts down or restarts the computer.
reboot	Restarts the system.
